': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-02T22:16:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James B. Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5a762e9-166a-58db-bddf-32a3c8c67127', 'word_count': 1366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5a762e9-166a-58db-bddf-32a3c8c67127'}, {'abstract': 'For the former Miss Universe from beauty pageant-crazy Venezuela, sexism and fat-shaming would be all too familiar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/03/opinion/alicia-machado-vs-donald-trumps-machismo.html', 'snippet': 'For the former Miss Universe from beauty pageant-crazy Venezuela, sexism and fat-shaming would be all too familiar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Venezuela isn’t only the country with the most Miss Universe winners, a whopping seven crowned since 1979. It is also fanatical about the contest in ways that run deep in the nation’s culture. The 1996 winner, Alicia Machado, with whom Donald J. Trump has been keeping up a very public feud, is considered a dissenter in that culture, in which she is both cherished and vilified.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/03/opinion/03stavans-inyt/03stavans-inyt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/03/opinion/03stavans-inyt/03stavans-inyt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub