n weight loss, “they often prescribe diets that make matters worse.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Doctors Treat Obese Patients', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-03T07:19:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/77a92cf1-a7b2-59d4-9160-396609b933ab', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77a92cf1-a7b2-59d4-9160-396609b933ab'}, {'abstract': 'We need more professionals in government, not fewer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/03/opinion/the-case-for-bureaucracy.html', 'snippet': 'We need more professionals in government, not fewer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a recent speech, Donald J. Trump revived a Republican plan to reduce the federal work force in part by replacing only some of the employees who retire as a way to pay for increased military spending and to avoid exceeding limits on the size of the federal budget. He also said he wanted to raise money by eliminating waste, collecting unpaid taxes and stopping improper tax payments.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case for Bureaucracy', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Case for Bureaucracy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Verkuil, Paul R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Evasion', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-03T07:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul R. Verkuil', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Verkuil', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba4f2676-427e-51b9-8789-f6aeec6cf031', 'word_count': 755, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba4f2676-427e-51b9-8789-f6aeec6cf031'}, {'abstract': 'Voluntary guidelines for self-driving cars are a good start, but the government will eventually need to develop mandatory regulations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/03/opinion/ushering-in-a-safe-driverless-future.html', 'snippet': 'Voluntary guidelines for self-driving cars are a good start, but the government will eventually need to develop mandatory regulations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The idea of getting into a car that drives itself — and may not even have a steering wheel — is enough to scare anyone who’s had a bad experience on the road or with technology. Those fears were made all the more real by a fatal crash involving a Tesla Model S that was traveling on autopilot in May. But if regulators and carmakers get it right, driverless cars have the potential to save tens of thousands of lives every year.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type