lue': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-03T07:21:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4343de54-5221-5cdf-bdf7-e49ab709f54d', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4343de54-5221-5cdf-bdf7-e49ab709f54d'}, {'abstract': 'The Nobel Prizes fail to recognize the seismic shifts science has undergone since the awards were conceived in the late 19th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/03/opinion/update-the-nobel-prizes.html', 'snippet': 'The Nobel Prizes fail to recognize the seismic shifts science has undergone since the awards were conceived in the late 19th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the mid-1960s, Robert Paine, a scientist at the University of Washington in Seattle, discovered a hidden organizing principle in the coastal ecosystem he was studying. When a certain species of starfish was present, a panoply of algae, limpets, barnacles, anemones and mussels lived in delicate, dynamic balance. But when he removed the starfish and tossed them into the ocean, that balance collapsed and one kind of mussel took over.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/03/opinion/03popkin/03popkin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/03/opinion/03popkin/03popkin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'