a49-30753cdb6876', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdb386b3-5380-583a-9a49-30753cdb6876'}, {'abstract': 'It seems you can, in fact, imagine the lives of people unlike yourself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/04/opinion/elena-ferrante-and-the-power-of-appropriation.html', 'snippet': 'It seems you can, in fact, imagine the lives of people unlike yourself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday, readers awoke to the news that one of the world’s outstanding literary mysteries may have finally been solved. In an article published on the website of The New York Review of Books, and simultaneously in French, German and Italian publications, the journalist Claudio Gatti wrote that Elena Ferrante — the pen name for an Italian novelist whose true identity was a closely guarded secret — is actually a Rome-based translator named Anita Raja. Using financial records, Mr. Gatti makes a seemingly irrefutable case that Ms. Raja is the author of the books that made the name Elena Ferrante famous around the world, including her four Neapolitan novels.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/04/opinion/04kirschWeb/04kirschWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/04/opinion/04kirschWeb/04kirschWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/04/opinion/04kirschWeb/04kirschWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/04/opinion/04kirschWeb/04kirschWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType':