: '2016-10-04T18:59:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c669ee0-5874-575c-8cae-58a482a86d5a', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c669ee0-5874-575c-8cae-58a482a86d5a'}, {'abstract': 'Something of a comeback for the celebrated chef; Akashi opens in Greenwich Village; and more restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/05/dining/david-burke-restaurant-upper-east-side.html', 'snippet': 'Something of a comeback for the celebrated chef; Akashi opens in Greenwich Village; and more restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAVERN 62 BY DAVID BURKE This represents something of a comeback for David Burke, a celebrated chef who dropped out of sight for several months while he dealt with business issues, even though his name was still on a number of restaurants. He has teamed up with ESquared Hospitality, and is opening this upscale tavern in the Upper East Side townhouse that housed his restaurant Fishtail, which closed last winter. In its new incarnation, the ground floor is the bar, where an afternoon menu will focus on toasts with various toppings. The second floor has a spacious dining room, a smaller room and a greenhouse garden room that will open later. Mr. Burke’s signature use of slabs of Himalayan pink salt is everywhere, in the food and even to cover some walls. His menu can be counted on for inventive touches; there are scallops with corned beef cheeks, lamb carbonara and Peking pork shank. The executive chef is Ed Cotton. (Opens Saturday): 135 East 62nd Street, 212-988-9021, tavern62.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{