Tribune: A Florida expert on the effects of light declares that television can cause lethargic behavior in children due to radiation beams, in October 1966.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — The lethargic behaviour of children who watch television many hours every day could be due partly to radiation beams from the picture tube, an experimenter said here today [Oct. 5]. A Florida nature photographer, Dr. John N. Ott, who has spent 15 years studying the sometimes weird effects of visible and invisible light on plants and animals, reported on his experiments at the 100th meeting of the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers. In one experiment, Dr. Ott placed three-month-old rats directly in front of a color television set. Within ten days, he said, rats protected only by black paper showed abnormal activity, and by 30 days, were extremely lethargic. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Oct. 6, 1966', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/blogs/06oldinyt-1966-TV/06oldinyt-1966-TV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/06/blogs/06oldinyt-1966-TV/06oldinyt-1966-TV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/blogs/06oldinyt-1966-TV/06oldinyt-1966-TV-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 197, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/06/blogs/06oldinyt-1966-TV/06oldinyt-1966-TV-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 197}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/blogs/06oldinyt-1966-TV/06oldinyt-1966-TV-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 276, 'width': 840, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_nam