 expected nationwide next month, Toys “R” Us and Babies “R” Us stores will have 529 plan gift cards available.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Parents are often reluctant to solicit gifts for their children’s college funds, even though they might prefer a gift of cash to another stuffed animal. But friends and relatives will soon have an option to contribute to a 529 saving account without being asked.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Want to Help Pay for a Child’s College? Grab a Gift Card', 'kicker': 'Your Money Adviser', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Want to Help Pay for a Child’s College? Grab a Gift Card', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carrns, Ann', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gift Cards and Certificates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Savings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Toys 'R' Us Inc", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gifts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-05T21:17:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea5deb30-bf8f-5460-b57f-5cafc61f9fcb', 'word_count': 851, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea5deb30-bf8f-5460-b57f-5cafc61f9fcb'}, {'abstract': 'James Turrell’s “Meeting,” opening on Saturday after a long renovation, will feature new computer-controlled LED auras for its cutout oculus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/06/arts/design/james-turrell-skyspace-an-artists-eye-to-the-sky-transformed-at-moma-ps1.html', 'snippet': 'James Turrell’s “Meeting,” opening on Saturday after a long renovation, will feature new computer-controlled LED auras for its cutout oculus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The three-year renovation of James Turrell’s beloved skyspace at MoMA PS1 might have tried even the patience of the Quakers who inspired it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06TURRELLJP/06TURRELLJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06TURRELLJP/06TURRELLJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 899}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06TURRELLJP/06TURRELLJP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 684, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06TURRELLJP/06TURRELLJP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1368, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06TURRELLJP/06TURRELLJP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06TURRELLJP/06TURRELLJP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06TURRELLJP/06TURRELLJP-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Artist’s Eye to the Sky, Transformed at MoMA PS1', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Artist’s Eye to the Sky Is About to Reopen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turrell, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MoMA PS1', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-05T21:22:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Barone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48b04e84-82fd-5981-82de-5e5fbbbad8a8', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48b04e84-82fd-5981-82de-5e5fbbbad8a8'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. French’s “The Trespasser” reunites two detectives to investigate the murder of a young woman whose Dream Date Barbie looks are part of the mystery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/06/books/review-tana-french-trespasser.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. French’s “The Trespasser” reunites two detectives to investigate the murder of a young woman whose Dream Date Barbie looks are part of the mystery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tana French, the superb Irish novelist who happens to write avidly about crime, used to link her books by having a minor character in one become the beleaguered protagonist of the next. Since the books all involved the Dublin Murder Squad, the beleaguered part came easily. But in her sixth novel, “The Trespasser,” she breaks that pattern to reunite the same pair of detectives who waded through “The Secret Place,” her fifth. That one took place at a swanky private school, a grating milieu where the girls’ teen language (“Um, duh?”) wasn’t easy for the detectives, Antoinette Conway and Stephen Moran, to take.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06BOOKFRENCH1/06BOOKFRENCH1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06BOOKFRENCH1/06BOOKFRENCH1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 785}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06BOOKFRENCH1/06BOOKFRENCH1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 784, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06BOOKFRENCH1/06BOOKFRENCH1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1267, 'width': 969, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06BOOKFRENCH1/06BOOKFRENCH1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06BOOKFRENCH1/06BOOKFRENCH1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06BOOKFRENCH1/06BOOKFRENCH1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: When Reading Tana French’s Ingenious New Novel, Assume Nothing', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Reading This Ingenious Murder Mystery, Assume Nothing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'French, Tana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Trespasser (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-05T21:23:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb2a69d9-e2f0-5c47-befe-107b9228d4a8', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb2a69d9-e2f0-5c47-befe-107b9228d4a8'}, {'abstract': 'The band’s new album, expounding on violence, death and lies, has it at war with the world yet again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/06/arts/music/green-day-revolution-radio-review.html', 'snippet': 'The band’s new album, expounding on violence, death and lies, has it at war with the world yet again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The state of the world is just too much for Green Day on “Revolution Radio.” Violence, war, death and lies loom everywhere; a glimmer of love or truth, or simply a respite, is the most the band can hope for, and it’s far from enough. But the tumult and desperation ignite the music on “Revolution Radio.” It’s the group’s first batch of new songs since “Uno! Dos! Tré!,” the three-disc surfeit of more straightforward tunes released in 2012. Those songs were built around snappy catchphrases and brisk, punky riffs. Green Day’s new ones aren’t so easily summed up, but they can roar through their contradictions.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/06/arts/06GREENDAY1/06GREENDAY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 