s.com/2016/10/07/arts/design/reimagining-the-divine-art-of-asia-at-the-philadelphia-museum-of-art.html', 'snippet': 'The museum has re-lit and recast its galleries devoted to South Asian art, and reopened them to the public. Here’s what to look for.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHILADELPHIA — The Philadelphia Museum of Art’s renowned collection of South Asian art had a problem: Despite a dazzling array of artifacts that thrilled scholars and absorbed curators, it made no sense to the general public.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07ASIANGALLERY1/07ASIANGALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07ASIANGALLERY1/07ASIANGALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07ASIANGALLERY1/07ASIANGALLERY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 605, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07ASIANGALLERY1/07ASIANGALLERY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1181, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07ASIANGALLERY1/07ASIANGALLERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07ASIANGALLERY1/07ASIANGALLERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07ASIANGALLERY1/07ASIANGALLERY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reimagining the Divine Art of Asia at the Philadelphia Museum of Art', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Redesigning  the Divine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hurdle, Jon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-06T22:02:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Hurdle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hurdle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2eaf02b7-31db-58a5-993f-77be6cb8d7c4', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2eaf02b7-31db-58a5-993f-77be6cb8d7c4'}, {'abstract': 'This version of the story returns to the popular, but discredited belief that dozens of people deliberately ignored the cries of Ms. Genovese.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/07/movies/37-review-kitty-genovese.html', 'snippet': 'This version of the story returns to the popular, but discredited belief that dozens of people deliberately ignored the cries of Ms. Genovese.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a conceit to Puk Grasten’s drama “37” that must be addressed up front. The film assumes that the oft-repeated circumstances surrounding the rape and murder of Kitty Genovese in Kew Gardens, Queens, in March 1964 are true — that 37 witnesses (actually, 38) did nothing to intervene. But the facts are otherwise, as “The Witness,” an effective documentary released in June that follows Ms. Genovese’s brother Bill as he investigates the crime, proved. The popular belief that neighbors ignored her cries, a notion, based on police claims, reported in a New York Times article at the time (and since reconsidered by the paper), was an exaggeration. Alas, a closing intertitle quotes the piece.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘37,’ a Fictional Retelling of the Kitty Genovese Story', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grasten, Puk', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': '37 (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Genovese, Kitty', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-06T22:06:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dc41b31-ef70-58a8-9a63-febd740bd0e4', 'word_count': 261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dc41b31-ef70-58a8-9a63-febd740bd0e4'}, {'abstract': 'The setting for this director’s lighthearted 20th feature is a stage production of “The Rainmaker,” whose cast is a hairbreadth from unemployment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/07/movies/ovation-review-henry-jaglom.html', 'snippet': 'The setting for this director’s lighthearted 20th feature is a stage production of “The Rainmaker,” whose cast is a hairbreadth from unemployment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Henry Jaglom’s movies are like artisanal cupcakes: The shape is sometimes weird, the flavors unpredictable, and the frosting occasionally overdone. Always reliable, though, are the baseline ingredients, and his 20th feature, “Ovation,” doesn’t disappoint. Dependably genuine, and suffused with Mr. Jaglom’s increasingly mellow intelligence, this lighthearted backstage drama will feel to his fans like a gathering of familiars.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07OVATION/07OVATION-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07OVATION/07OVATION-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07OVATION/07OVATION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07OVATION/07OVATION-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1111, 'width': 1977, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07OVATION/07OVATION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07OVATION/07OVATION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07OVATION/07OVATION-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In Henry Jaglom’s ‘Ovation,’ the Action Is Backstage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jaglom, Henry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ovation (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frederick, Tanna', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-06T22:11:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/86376825-93b3-5e44-ba47-c37f82003af6', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86376825-93b3-5e44-ba47-c37f82003af6'}, {'abstract': 'Fine leading performances lift this film, which is based on a Punjab folk tale of star-crossed lovers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/07/movies/mirzya-review.html', 'snippet': 'Fine leading performances lift this film, which is based on a Punjab folk tale of star-crossed lovers.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Mirzya” is one gorgeously shot movie, and the filmmakers slow down some scenes to make sure you know it. They needn’t have bothered. Most everything in this easy-to-like story is hard to miss.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07MIRZYA/07MIRZYA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07MIRZYA/07MIRZYA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07MIRZYA/07MIRZYA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07MIRZYA/07MIRZYA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07MIRZYA/07MIRZYA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07MIRZYA/07MIRZYA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/arts/07MIRZYA/07MIRZYA-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Mirzya,’ Echoes of ‘Romeo and Juliet,’ Lushly Shot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mehra, Rakeysh Omprakash', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mirzya (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-06T22:15:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Jaworowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaworowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d963771-78d9-54f4-a002-247f33ff6aea', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d963771-78d9-54f4-a002-247f33ff6aea'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading by book critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/09/books/review/8-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading by book critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest round of books we like are meaty, weighty, often serious — and very, very good. This week, in an exciting new addition to the list, we’re incorporating favorites from our daily book critics, Michiko Kakutani, Dwight Garner and Jennifer Senior, all of whom bring their enthusiasms to bear on what they’ve read recently, and all of whom have piqued my interest in what they’ve been reading.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/books/review/09edchoice-1/09edchoice-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/09/books/review/09edchoice-1/09edchoice-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/books/review/09edchoice-1/09edchoice-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/books/review/09edchoice-1/09edchoice-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 209, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/09/books/review/09edchoice-1/09edchoice-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 209}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/books/review/09edchoice-1/09edchoice-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/books/review/09edchoice-1/09edchoice-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 1293, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '8 New Books We Recommend This Week', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-06T22:17:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6025acbe-6755-5329-8490-204b6129d186', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6025acbe-6755-5329-8490-204b6129d186'}, {'abstract': 'Lacy Harber, a wealthy Texas businessman, took out ads in four papers to denounce the bank, which is under fire over its phony accounts scandal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/07/business/after-congressional-grilling-wells-fargo-is-berated-in-newspaper-ads.html', 'snippet': 'Lacy Harber, a wealthy Texas businessman, took out ads in four papers to denounce the bank, which is under fire over its phony accounts scandal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Former bank tellers and angry customers. Democrats and Republicans in Congress. State treasurers in California and Illinois. Even Hillary Clinton. They have all lambasted Wells Fargo in recent weeks over the phony accounts scandal.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/07/business/07DB-WELLS3-1475791810548/07DB-WELLS-1475791810548-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/07/business/07DB