None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f157112-9ca9-56f1-bb54-986810085ddf', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f157112-9ca9-56f1-bb54-986810085ddf'}, {'abstract': 'The country has hundreds of local variations on language, each with its own quirks of pronunciation, inflection and vocabulary, which can make daily interactions confusing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/08/world/what-in-the-world/italy-dialects.html', 'snippet': 'The country has hundreds of local variations on language, each with its own quirks of pronunciation, inflection and vocabulary, which can make daily interactions confusing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Say you’re shopping at a farmer’s market in Rome, and you’d like to pick up some nice, ripe watermelon. The signs at some stands call it “anguria”; others say “cocomero” or “melone d’acqua.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/14/world/what-in-the-world/00wit_italy-dialects/00wit_italy-dialects-articleLarge-v2.gif', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/14/world/what-in-the-world/00wit_italy-dialects/00wit_italy-dialects-articleLarge-v2.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/14/world/what-in-the-world/00wit_italy-dialects/00wit_italy-dialects-jumbo-v2.gif', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/14/world/what-in-the-world/00wit_italy-dialects/00wit_italy-dialects-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/14/world/what-in-the-world/00wit_italy-dialects/00wit_italy-dialects-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/14/world/what-in-the-world/00wit_italy-dialects/00wit_italy-dialects-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/14/world/what-in-the-world/00wit_italy-dialects/00wit_italy-dialects-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'You Say ‘Anguria,’ I Say ‘Cocomero’: Italy’s Many Dialects', 'kicker': 'What in the World', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Italian Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-07T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'What in the World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gaia Pianigiani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gaia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pianigiani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b8f6d80-526f-579e-bba9-9719e4dae99e', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b8f6d80-526f-579e-bba9-9719e4dae99e'}, {'abstract': 'Lie logically. Avoid amateurs and rich people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/09/magazine/how-to-bluff-at-cards.html', 'snippet': 'Lie logically. Avoid amateurs and rich people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Build lies on logical narratives. “You can’t be telling a murder mystery that all of a sudden ends as a comedy,” says Chris Moneymaker, who as an amateur bluffed his way to the $2.5 million first-place prize at the World Series of Poker Main Event in 2003 with what one ESPN commentator called “the bluff of the century.” In poker, a bluff is a bet or raise that gives the false impression of a stronger hand. Bet erratically — in ways that are inconsistent with your pattern of play in previous hands or that don’t make sense given the cards on the table — and your “story” becomes irrational, which will pique opponents’ curiosity and prompt them to call you out.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/magazine/09tip/09mag-09tip-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/09/magazine/09tip/09mag-09tip-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/magazine/09tip/09mag-09tip-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/magazine/09tip/09mag-09tip-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 591, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/09/magazine/09tip/09mag-09tip-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 591}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/magazine/09tip/09mag-09tip-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1009, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '