ord_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9300e2bd-766e-5220-a45f-ccc44d8d1888'}, {'abstract': 'David E. Kelley’s new legal series represents a change of venue from broadcast TV. Making it for Amazon, he says, means “characters can have more time and room.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/09/arts/television/billy-bob-thornton-plays-a-lawyer-in-goliath-a-drunkard-too.html', 'snippet': 'David E. Kelley’s new legal series represents a change of venue from broadcast TV. Making it for Amazon, he says, means “characters can have more time and room.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'SANTA MONICA, CALIF. — Say what you will about Ally McBeal — and, over the years, people have said a lot — she was a likable character, or at least she was intended to be. Same with Bobby Donnell, the morally squishy senior partner on “The Practice,” and the womanizing Alan Shore, of both “The Practice” and “Boston Legal.” David E. Kelley, the creator and prolific writer of many of TV’s most celebrated legal dramas, made them that way, partly for his own sanity.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/watching/109GOLIATH1/109GOLIATH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/09/watching/109GOLIATH1/109GOLIATH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/watching/109GOLIATH1/109GOLIATH1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/09/watching/109GOLIATH1/109GOLIATH1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1251, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo'