one, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e0bd8c8-7adb-55ce-a338-92e94e5dc575', 'word_count': 233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e0bd8c8-7adb-55ce-a338-92e94e5dc575'}, {'abstract': 'This event at New York City Center lasted only two weeks but was a cornucopia of works by new artists and renowned names like Alvin Ailey.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/10/arts/dance/fall-for-dance-festival-is-short-but-packed.html', 'snippet': 'This event at New York City Center lasted only two weeks but was a cornucopia of works by new artists and renowned names like Alvin Ailey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s begin this account of Fall for Dance’s final two programs just by dropping names. Alessandra Ferri and Herman Cornejo, international ballet celebrities, danced a world premiere by Wayne McGregor on Friday and Saturday nights. On Wednesday and Thursday night, Alina Cojocaru, Friedemann Vogel and Johan Kobborg, names of equal ballet consequence, danced a one-act drama by Frederick Ashton not seen complete in New York since 2004. Friday’s program began with an extended solo by Shantala Shivalingappa, one of the most renowned of all Indian classical dancers. The Wednesday-Thursday fare also included another remarkable one-woman vehicle, Alvin Ailey’s full-length solo “Cry.” The list is extraordinary.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10FALLFORDANCE-REFER/10FALLFORDANCE-REFER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10FALLFORDANCE-REFER/10FALLFORDANCE-REFER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 668}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cre