pe_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e8641ad-4046-5a31-afaf-5433a4f7504f', 'word_count': 1030, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e8641ad-4046-5a31-afaf-5433a4f7504f'}, {'abstract': 'Gustavo Dudamel led the orchestra in three demanding programs to open Carnegie Hall’s season. But difficult questions hover over the orchestra’s artistic endeavors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/10/arts/music/review-simon-bolivar-symphony-orchestras-evolution-raises-issues-of-mission.html', 'snippet': 'Gustavo Dudamel led the orchestra in three demanding programs to open Carnegie Hall’s season. But difficult questions hover over the orchestra’s artistic endeavors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2007, in a breakthrough appearance, the Simón Bolívar Youth Symphony Orchestra of Venezuela played two programs at Carnegie Hall. It felt as though an inspiring roster of dedicated, eager young artists, against great odds, had come from South America to jolt classical music with fresh purpose and energy. Under Gustavo Dudamel, their charismatic conductor, 26 at the time, they delivered electrifying performances of Beethoven’s Fifth Symphony and Bartok’s Concerto for Orchestra, among other works.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10DUDAMEL/10DUDAMEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10DUDAMEL/10DUDAMEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10DUDAMEL/10DUDAMEL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10DUDAMEL/10DUDAMEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10DUDAMEL/10DUDAMEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10DUDAMEL/10DUDAMEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10DUDAMEL/10DUDAMEL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Simón Bolívar Symphony Orchestra’s Evolution Raises Issues of Mission', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Orchestra’s Evolution Raises Issues of Mission', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dudamel, Gustavo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bolivar, Simon, Youth Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'El Sistema', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-09T22:15:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f6a0da5-9240-5ccd-bcca-22f0c5d437f4', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f6a0da5-9240-5ccd-bcca-22f0c5d437f4'}, {'abstract': 'The festival, which runs through Oct. 16, tends to front-load its big premieres, but it has grown so large that attendees can still find plenty to watch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/10/movies/a-guide-to-enjoying-the-final-stretch-of-the-new-york-film-festival.html', 'snippet': 'The festival, which runs through Oct. 16, tends to front-load its big premieres, but it has grown so large that attendees can still find plenty to watch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every festival has its own agenda, pulse and profile. Cannes rolls out the biggest red carpet, Sundance turns newbies into indie stars and Toronto cranks up the new fall season with world premieres. The New York Film Festival has institutional heft — it’s presented by the Film Society of Lincoln Center — and an advantageous spot on the fall calendar, which allows it to present hotly anticipated new titles in North America before they’ve become old media news. And of course the festival also has New York, where audiences still turn movies into kinetic social events.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10NYFFWEEK3-1/10NYFFWEEK3-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10NYFFWEEK3-1/10NYFFWEEK3-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 902}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/10NYFFWEEK3-1/10NYFFWEEK3-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/arts/1