a demographic they had long dismissed as a punch line — low-income, uneducated whites in economically depleted regions — he awakened them to the fact that the groovy progressive social values they had assumed were a national fait accompli were actually only half the story.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/1016-BKS-Egos/1016-BKS-Egos-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/1016-BKS-Egos/1016-BKS-Egos-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1000}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/1016-BKS-Egos/1016-BKS-Egos-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 615, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/1016-BKS-Egos/1016-BKS-Egos-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1229, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/1016-BKS-Egos/1016-BKS-Egos-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/1016-BKS-Egos/1016-BKS-Egos-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/1016-BKS-Egos/1016-BKS-Egos-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Memoirs Show How the Other Half Lives', 'kicker': 'Egos', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Memoir', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daum, Meghan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vance, J D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ensign, Josephine (1960- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dykeman, Wilma', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Hillbilly Elegy: A Memoir of a Family and Culture in Crisis (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Catching Homelessness: A Nurse's Story of Falling Through the Safety Net (Book)", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Family of Earth: A Southern Mountain Childhood (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-10T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meghan Daum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meghan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6531f744-1d56-5b89-ae23-66234d9aefcc', 'word_count': 1379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6531f744-1d56-5b89-ae23-66234d9aefcc'}, {'abstract': 'Why are we so concerned with bacteria related to the five-second rule? Other surfaces are far more dangerous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/11/upshot/im-a-doctor-if-i-drop-food-on-the-kitchen-floor-i-still-eat-it.html', 'snippet': 'Why are we so concerned with bacteria related to the five-second rule? Other surfaces are far more dangerous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You may have read or heard about the study debunking the five-second rule. It said that no matter how fast you pick up food that fal