'snippet': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: Steel mills struggle to fulfill mass orders in Pittsburg and Chicago in 1891.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steel mills are reported to be more concerned about inability to make appreciable headway against the mass of orders already upon the books than in obtaining new contracts. Domestic consumers’ needs for the first half of next year are far from being satisfied, and the foreign demand for commercial forms would take up a large, additional tonnage if mills could promise shipments before July, 1917. Russia continues negotiating for 60,000 tons of barbed wire, and the closing of contracts for 7,500 more railroad carriages, requiring 75,000 tons of steel, is imminent. The great activity was in the Pittsburg and Chicago districts. — The New York Herald, European Edition, Oct. 11, 1891', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/blogs/10iht-retrospective-steel/10iht-retrospective-steel-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/10/blogs/10iht-retrospective-steel/10iht-retrospective-steel-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/blogs/10iht-retrospective-steel/10iht-retrospective-steel-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/blogs/10iht-retrospective-steel/10iht-retrospective-steel-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/10/blogs/10iht-retrospective-steel/10iht-retrospective-steel-