rticle', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Weed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89e0e9d3-cad5-5236-9cc8-9152307103a3', 'word_count': 1043, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89e0e9d3-cad5-5236-9cc8-9152307103a3'}, {'abstract': 'Extensive consumer databases are not in place for lenders to check, but companies are scrambling to crack the credit code.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/11/business/international/snooping-in-the-bathroom-to-assess-credit-risk-in-china.html', 'snippet': 'Extensive consumer databases are not in place for lenders to check, but companies are scrambling to crack the credit code.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KUNSHAN, China — Banks and other lenders typically look at borrowers’ credit histories, tax forms and other financial information to determine whether they will get paid back. In China, lenders also look at their bathrooms.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/05/business/05chinacredit-illo/05chinacredit-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/05/business/05chinacredit-illo/05chinacredit-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/05/business/05chinacredit-illo/05chinacredit-illo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/05/business/05chinacredit-illo