tion', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Russell', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-12T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Ana Marie Cox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Ana', 'lastname': 'Marie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab13121b-298d-5e10-9fe5-bf1ac9ed8984', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab13121b-298d-5e10-9fe5-bf1ac9ed8984'}, {'abstract': 'Like many of my friends, I spent years using prescription stimulants to get through school and start my career. Then I tried to get off them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/magazine/generation-adderall-addiction.html', 'snippet': 'Like many of my friends, I spent years using prescription stimulants to get through school and start my career. Then I tried to get off them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have you ever been to Enfield? I had never even heard of it until I was 23 and living in London for graduate school. One afternoon, I received notification that a package whose arrival I had been anticipating for days had been bogged down in customs and was now in a FedEx warehouse in Enfield, an unremarkable London suburb. I was outside my flat within minutes of receiving this news and on the train to Enfield within the hour, staring through the window at the gray sky. The package in question, sent from Los Angeles, contained my monthly supply of Adderall.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/magazine/16adderall1-copy/16adderall1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': '