bType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/05/t-magazine/05tmag-benech-slide-EGUI/05tmag-benech-slide-EGUI-articleLarge.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/05/t-magazine/05tmag-benech-slide-EGUI/05tmag-benech-slide-EGUI-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/05/t-magazine/05tmag-benech-slide-EGUI/05tmag-benech-slide-EGUI-jumbo.png', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/05/t-magazine/05tmag-benech-slide-EGUI/05tmag-benech-slide-EGUI-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Louis Benech Masterpiece: Mas Sainte-Anne', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-10-12T16:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/72a7a82d-ec55-535a-b94b-a02ad87cc122', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/72a7a82d-ec55-535a-b94b-a02ad87cc122'}, {'abstract': 'Louis Benech looks back on his seminal projects, testaments to his glorious gift and to the constant, occasionally heart-rending, effects of time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/12/t-magazine/design/louis-benech-french-gardens.html', 'snippet': 'Louis Benech looks back on his seminal projects, testaments to his glorious gift and to the constant, occasionally heart-rending, effects of time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gardens are “an expression of faith” and “the embodiment of hope,” wrote the revered English landscape architect Russell Page in his memoir, “The Education of a Gardener,” in 1962. If you’re lucky, you start with a blank slate and some time in the future, years later, it may become something like what you had in mind. Gardens are forever evolving and growing, like children to adulthood, and like all things in nature, they can be hobbled by disease, or refashioned for the times or, on occasion, brutally destroyed before reaching maturity — life interrupted. They hold a beauty and a sadness — seasonally, of course, from those early sprouts pushing through the softened earth of spring, to the autumn, when the leaves fade and fall and the barrenness of winter sets in, but also in their impermanence. Gardens continually remind us of our mortality.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '108', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/t-magazine/10tmag-gardentop/10tmag-gardentop-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/10/t-magazine/10tmag-gardentop/10tmag-gardentop-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/t-magazine/10tmag-gardentop/10tmag-gardentop-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/t-magazine/10tmag-gardentop/10tmag-gardentop-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/t-magazine/10tmag-gardentop/10tmag-gardentop-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/10/t-magazine/10tmag-gardentop/10tmag-gardentop-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/10/t-magazine/10tmag-gardentop/10tmag-gardentop-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gardens by France’s Most Revered Landscape Designer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How His Gardens Grew ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Dana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-12T16:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Design & Interiors', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1288e71-412c-5171-b110-7d37d34183dd', 'word_count': 1663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1288e71-412c-5171-b110-7d37d34183dd'}, {'abstract': '“Climax” explores the chaos, intimacy and experimental leanings of Yasmeen Godder’s 15-year career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/arts/dance/gibney-dance-a-polarizing-choreographer-from-israel-returns.html', 'snippet': '“Climax” explores the chaos, intimacy and experimental leanings of Yasmeen Godder’s 15-year career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the past decade or so, Israel has emerged as an exciting hub of contemporary dance with a physical style of extreme precision, palpable tension and sexy aggression. But Yasmeen Godder has embraced the awkward and the grotesque instead, becoming one of the country’s most important, and polarizing, choreographers. While other artists exert control, she celebrates chaos: To watch one of her shows is to watch a world slide into anarchy.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD2/1016WEEKAHEAD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD2/1016WEEKAHEAD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD2/1016WEEKAHEAD2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD2/1016WEEKAHEAD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD2/1016WEEKAHEAD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD2/1016WEEKAHEAD2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD2/1016WEEKAHEAD2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1331, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gibney Dance: A Polarizing Choreographer From Israel Returns', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance; Immersive and Intimate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gibney Dance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Godder, Yasmeen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Climax (Dance)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-12T16:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Schaefer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaefer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c04ce324-0ba7-5bf8-bd91-e57ff9af1ec9', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c04ce324-0ba7-5bf8-bd91-e57ff9af1ec9'}, {'abstract': 'The quartet is observing its 70th anniversary, while Mr. Zorn is receiving an expansive tribute.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/arts/music/celebrations-the-juilliard-string-quartet-and-john-zorn.html', 'snippet': 'The quartet is observing its 70th anniversary, while Mr. Zorn is receiving an expansive tribute.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now containing no one who played with any of its original members, the still-glorious Juilliard String Quartet celebrates its 70th anniversary on Monday, Oct. 17, at Alice Tully Hall with its current complement: Joseph Lin and Ronald Copes, violins; Roger Tapping, viola; and Astrid Schween, cello. The program consists of two middle-period Beethoven quartets and Bartok’s moody Quartet No. 1. (7:30 p.m., juilliard.edu.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': 