_date': '2016-10-12T16:03:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/378c71f1-74bd-57ea-9502-003ea273c695', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/378c71f1-74bd-57ea-9502-003ea273c695'}, {'abstract': '“Letters to a Man” looks at Vaslav Nijinsky in a performance piece from Robert Wilson.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/theater/mikhail-baryshnikov-letters-to-a-man.html', 'snippet': '“Letters to a Man” looks at Vaslav Nijinsky in a performance piece from Robert Wilson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to W. H. Auden, “What mad Nijinsky wrote about Diaghilev is true of the normal heart.” Empathetic theatergoers have a tantalizing opportunity to test Auden’s assertion this month, as one of the greatest male dancers of the 20th century, Mikhail Baryshnikov, interprets the tormented words of another, Vaslav Nijinsky, in “Letters to a Man” (the man being Nijinsky’s lover and mentor, the Russian impresario Sergei Diaghilev), which runs Oct. 15 to Oct. 30 at the Brooklyn Academy of Music.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD5/1016WEEKAHEAD5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD5/1016WEEKAHEAD5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD5/1016WEEKAHEAD5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD5/1016WEEKAHEAD5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1285, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD5/1016WEEKAHEAD5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD5/1016WEEKAHEAD5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/1016WEEKAHEAD5/1016WEEKAHEAD5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mikhail Baryshnikov Prepares to Portray, What Else, a Dancer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater; Dancer Plays a Dancer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Letters to a Man (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Robert (1941- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baryshnikov, Mikhail', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nijinsky, Vaslav', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-12T16:04:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d0e69eb-d92b-5b9e-a480-4cb9c6ef6455', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d0e69eb-d92b-5b9e-a480-4cb9c6ef6455'}, {'abstract': 'Cary Ann Hearst and Michael Trent, the married couple who make up this band, will perform at the Music Hall of Williamsburg and the Bowery Ballroom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/arts/music/shovels-rope-visiting-new-york-with-a-scrappy-new-album.html', 'snippet': 'Cary Ann Hearst and Michael Trent, the married couple who make up this band, will perform at the Music Hall of Williamsburg and the Bowery Ballroom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cary Ann Hearst and Michael Trent, the married couple who make up Shovels & Rope, share a belief in the power of raw emotion. Their scrappy new album, “Little Seeds” (New West), draws loosely from folk rock, cowpunk and Southern gothic traditions, with stories to spare. “Mourning Song” is an intimate reflection on the toll of Alzheimer’s. “BWYR” proposes a shaky but honest unity in the face of mass violence. But Mr. Trent, typically heard strumming a guitar, and Ms. Hearst, chiefly the band’s drummer, can deliver as much punch with a lighter premise, singing in their rafters-raising harmony. “I Know” suggests a sneering put-down of a rival band’s pretensions, while “San Andreas Fault Line Blues” belongs to the ageless category of Los Angeles disillusionment narratives. (Music Hall of Williamsburg, Monday, Oct. 17; Bowery Ballroom, Oct. 18-19.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shovels & Rope, Visiting New York With a Scrappy New Album', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop; Telling Stories in Scrappy Songs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shovels & Rope (Music Group)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Little Seeds (Album)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-12T16:05:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3456a17f-95a4-589c-9693-6a4bbf67e666', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3456a17f-95a4-589c-9693-6a4bbf67e666'}, {'abstract': 'The Business Roundtable has strongly endorsed a link between board diversity and board effectiveness and the creation of long-term shareholder value.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/14/business/dealbook/corporate-board-diversity-gets-push-from-business-leaders.html', 'snippet': 'The Business Roundtable has strongly endorsed a link between board diversity and board effectiveness and the creation of long-term shareholder value.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The job of the board nominating committee of large corporations just got harder. Chief executives have laid down a new marker that substantially increases the emphasis on promoting diversity in corporate boardrooms.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corporate Board Diversity Gets Push From Business Leaders', 'kicker': 'Another View', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Business Roundtable', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-12T16:07:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael W. Peregrine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peregrine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df99102c-dc8e-52c8-9f83-9e10466e2a85', 'word_count': 843, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df99102c-dc8e-52c8-9f83-9e10466e2a85'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Cave’s installation “Until” addresses recent lethal incidents of racial violence on an epic scale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/arts/design/nick-cave-sculptural-and-political-at-mass-moca.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Cave’s installation “Until” addresses recent lethal incidents of racial violence on an epic scale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nick Cave’s “Soundsuits,” wearable sculptural assemblages of fantastic complexity, are so compelling that it’s easy to forget they began as political protest. He made the first one in response to the 1991 Los Angeles police beating of Rodney King. Mr. Cave’s new installation at Mass MoCA, in North Adams, Mass., is political, too. It addresses more recent lethal incidents of racial violence — the killings of Michael Brown, Eric Garner and Trayvon Martin — on an epic scale. Titled “Until” — as in “guilty until proven innocent” — the installation (opening Saturday, Oct. 15) fills the museum’s football-field-size main space, functioning as a kind of walk-in soundsuit, composed of thousands of eye-catching found objects. But Mr. Cave cuts the high spirits with images of guns, bullets and targets. Besides being sculptural, the piece is architectural, meant to serve as an assembly hall/stage, where dancers, singers and poets will perform and community groups meet during the show’s yearlong run. (massmoca.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'captio