person': [{'firstname': 'Margot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanger-Katz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5b402db-90ab-5cb0-832c-850a0003d28c', 'word_count': 1114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5b402db-90ab-5cb0-832c-850a0003d28c'}, {'abstract': 'During the Texas winter of 1870, a war veteran who works as a news reader agrees to bring a rescued orphan to San Antonio to find her closest living relatives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/13/books/news-of-the-world-paulette-jiles.html', 'snippet': 'During the Texas winter of 1870, a war veteran who works as a news reader agrees to bring a rescued orphan to San Antonio to find her closest living relatives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paulette Jiles was a poet before she became a novelist. And it certainly shows. Her new novel, the 2016 National Book Award nominee “News of the World,” has invited comparisons with both Charles Portis’s “True Grit” (because it involves a girl on a long journey with an older man) and John Ford’s film “The Searchers” (because it involves a man’s journey to “rescue” a white girl who has been kidnapped by Indians, and also involves a long journey). But it’s more like Ms. Jiles’s own “Enemy Women,” an exquisitely written Civil War epic about a woman’s long march to find her lover, or any fiction by Ron Rash, another poet who chooses each word with expert precision.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/13/arts/13BOOKJILES/13BOOKJILES-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/13/arts/13BOOKJILES/13BOOKJILES-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/13/arts/13BOOKJILES/13BOOKJILES-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/13/arts/13BOOKJILES/13BOOKJILES-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/13/arts/13BOOKJILES/13BOOKJILES-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 778}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/13/arts/13BOOKJILES/13BOOKJILES-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 790, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/13/arts/13BOOKJILES/13BOOKJILES-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1483, 'width': 1143, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘News of the World,’ Unlikely Companions Bond on a Perilous Journey', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unlikely Companions Bond on a Most Perilous Journey', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'News of the World (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jiles, Paulette', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-12T21:13:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d677535d-b9ed-5a5c-9491-df225fe84a9c', 'word_count': 898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d677535d-b9ed-5a5c-9491-df225fe84a9c'}, {'abstract': 'A researcher’s lucky discovery of a fossilized vocal organ offers new insights into sounds heard when dinosaurs ruled the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/13/science/oldest-bird-voice-box-syrinx.html', 'snippet': 'A researcher’s lucky discovery of a fossilized vocal organ offers new insights into sounds heard when dinosaurs ruled the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Jurassic Park movies portrayed a prehistoric soundscape filled with brachiosaurus bellows, velociraptor shrieks and Tyrannosaurus rex roars. But because paleontologists have never found fossilized vocal organs from any of those dinosaurs, we don’t really know what their world sounded like.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/13/science/13TB-bird/13TB-bird-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/13/science/13TB-bird/13TB-bird-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/13/science/13TB-bird/13TB-bird-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/13/science/13TB-bird/13TB-bird-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 783, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/13/science/13TB-bird/13TB-bird-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 783}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type