ion_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Rojas and Kate Pastor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rojas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pastor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/223e1e36-6e8e-5bec-9593-fe787b1392e5', 'word_count': 1117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/223e1e36-6e8e-5bec-9593-fe787b1392e5'}, {'abstract': 'From the backstage sanctuary at a drag club to defiant protests on the street, Joana Toro has documented life along La Roosevelt, the center of New York’s Latino L.G.B.T. community.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/10/13/life-in-the-heart-of-new-yorks-lgbt-latino-community-joana-toro/', 'snippet': 'From the backstage sanctuary at a drag club to defiant protests on the street, Joana Toro has documented life along La Roosevelt, the center of New York’s Latino L.G.B.T. community.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roosevelt Avenue, in Jackson Heights, Queens, reminds my boyfriend of El Salvador, where he grew up. It reminds our friend Danny of where he grew up, in Mexico. And others of Colombia or Guatemala. That’s fitting, because it’s one of the vibrant centers of Latino culture in New York. A walk down La Roosevelt, as some call it, means taco trucks on every block, norteño and bachata music wailing from the pollo asado joints and barber shops that never close, and Spanish everywhere.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Life in the Heart of New York’s L.G.B.T. Latino Community', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sightseer in the Temple', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 