unted Houses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Army Terminal (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-13T20:44:57+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Theater', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/940875e6-7b52-5a33-9ee5-52c71451a778', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/940875e6-7b52-5a33-9ee5-52c71451a778'}, {'abstract': 'You know that Pedro Reyes’s political house of horrors in the Brooklyn Army Terminal is theater, but some acts make your pulse jump and disarm your defenses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/14/arts/design/review-doomocracy-looms-at-fright-night-in-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'You know that Pedro Reyes’s political house of horrors in the Brooklyn Army Terminal is theater, but some acts make your pulse jump and disarm your defenses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Halloween started early this year. I’d put the date at July 18, opening night of the 2016 Republican National Convention. Trick-or-treating has been nonstop since. The tricks have included a couple of Creature Feature debates, email scandals, xenophobic rants and personal attacks, all of which have too often been received as sordid but tantalizing treats by audience and news media alike.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At ‘Doomocracy,’ It’s Fright Night in Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fright Night in Brooklyn: The Election Edition', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reyes, Pedro (1972- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Creative Time', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Army Terminal (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-13T20:45:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d796715f-a2f2-5a82-9527-655e6d69ffa5', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d796715f-a2f2-5a82-9527-655e6d69ffa5'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading by book critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/books/review/7-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading by book critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s literary award season and publishers are rushing out editions of books that have been longlisted and shortlisted for the season’s prizes. Two of the books on our list of recommendations this week fit that category: David Szalay’s “All That Man Is” is on the shortlist for the Man Booker Prize; the winner will be announced Oct. 25. And Cathy O’Neil’s “Weapons of Math Destruction” was longlisted for the National Book Award in nonfiction. It didn’t make the final cut for the shortlist — but we still like it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/16edchoice-1/16edchoice-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 287, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/16/books/review/16edchoice-1/16edchoice-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewi