original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7cd76b9-4817-5c3c-82ec-61cf113dc0fd', 'word_count': 845, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7cd76b9-4817-5c3c-82ec-61cf113dc0fd'}, {'abstract': 'Voters are being held hostage by hollow promises from both parties. The Republicans have a chance to win back their trust.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/14/opinion/erick-erickson-the-gop-after-donald-trump.html', 'snippet': 'Voters are being held hostage by hollow promises from both parties. The Republicans have a chance to win back their trust.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATLANTA — Over the past few months, a cavalcade of Republican policy wonks, pundits and partisan strategists have beclowned themselves on national television trying to defend the indefensible: their party’s nominee. Free-trade advocates have become protectionists. Strategists are treating Drudge Report surveys as scientific endeavors while dismissing legitimate polls as Clintonian propaganda.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Erick Erickson: The G.O.P. After Donald Trump', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Trump', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erickson, Erick-Woods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-14T07:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erick-Woods Erickson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erick-woods', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erickson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b6319b5-f457-53cd-a6ef-692e69a614ee', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b6319b5-f457-53cd-a6ef-692e69a614ee'}, {'abstract': 'A large margin of victory will be needed for an effective presidency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/14/opinion/the-clinton-agenda.html', 'snippet': 'A large margin of victory will be needed for an effective presidency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It ain’t over until the portly gentleman screams, but it is, as intelligence analysts say, highly likely that Hillary Clinton will win this election. Poll-based models put her chances at around 90 percent earlier this week — and that was before the campaign turned totally X-rated.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/14/opinion/14krugmanWeb/14krugmanWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/14/opinion/14krugmanWeb/14krugmanWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/14/opinion/14krugmanWeb/14krugmanWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/14/opinion/14krugmanWeb/14krugmanWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'imag