}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The 10.2.16 Issue', 'kicker': 'Thread', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 10.2.16 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-10-14T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9648c15e-6e33-5d28-87c0-a29e674b3b9f', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9648c15e-6e33-5d28-87c0-a29e674b3b9f'}, {'abstract': 'Do you find yourself surrounded by people who are staring at their screens instead of having face-to-face conversations? Are you ever guilty of doing that, too?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/14/learning/does-technology-make-us-more-alone.html', 'snippet': 'Do you find yourself surrounded by people who are staring at their screens instead of having face-to-face conversations? Are you ever guilty of doing that, too?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Technology is supposed to make us more connected. We can stay in touch with our friends all the time on Facebook, Instagram and Snapchat, and, of course, by texting. But are our smartphones actually getting in the way of real socializing? Could technology be making us more alone?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Technology Make Us More Alone?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-10-14T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4b3e1e1-da83-58e1-951e-c68baf22eda3', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4b3e1e1-da83-58e1-951e-c68baf22eda3'}, {'abstract': 'Through her spare, often uncomfortably observant movies, the filmmaker tells the stories of women moving through an uneasy world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/magazine/the-quiet-menace-of-kelly-reichardts-feminist-westerns.html', 'snippet': 'Through her spare, often uncomfortably observant movies, the filmmaker tells the stories of women moving through an uneasy world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Walking through a rapidly gentrifying neighborhood of Portland, Ore., in July, Kelly Reichardt noticed a group of teenage street punks. They had dreadlocks and dogs and dirty clothing emblazoned with hand-drawn anarchy symbols. Reichardt faltered for a moment, and then crossed the street. She told me that she suspected one of them had begun shooting up on a garage roof next door to the house where she was staying. “My instinct is to call the cops,” she said. “But then it’s like, God, whose side am I on?”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/magazine/16reichardt1/16mag-16reichardt-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/16/magazine/16reichardt1/16mag-16reichardt-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 656}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/magazine/16reichardt1/16mag-16reichardt-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 936, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/magazine/16reichardt1/16mag-16reichardt-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1371, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name':