e. It seemed to depict George and Willie Muse, the brothers around whom “Truevine” revolves, sitting with shackles binding their legs to their chairs. Ms. Macy glimpsed this photo during a late-night web search, but when she called it up again the next day she realized she’d been mistaken.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17BOOKMACY/17BOOKMACY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 857, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17BOOKMACY/17BOOKMACY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 857}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17BOOKMACY/17BOOKMACY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 718, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17BOOKMACY/17BOOKMACY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1411, 'width': 988, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17BOOKMACY/17BOOKMACY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17BOOKMACY/17BOOKMACY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17BOOKMACY/17BOOKMACY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: An Account of Black Albino Brothers in Beth Macy’s ‘Truevine’', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_ki