, about a woman who faces a changing reality when she returns to her childhood home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/17/theater/the-cherry-orchard-review.html', 'snippet': 'Diane Lane stars in this terminally confused production of Chekhov’s play, about a woman who faces a changing reality when she returns to her childhood home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toward the welcome end of the Roundabout Theater Company’s terminally confused production of Chekhov’s “The Cherry Orchard,” a character who has just exhausted himself by dancing like James Brown on steroids laments, “Oh, if only we could move faster through this next part.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17CHERRY/17CHERRY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17CHERRY/17CHERRY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17CHERRY/17CHERRY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17CHERRY/17CHERRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17CHERRY/17CHERRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17CHERRY/17CHERRY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/17/arts/17CHERRY/17CHERRY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: All’s Not Well in This ‘Cherry Orchard’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Returning Home, and All’s Not Well', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Cherry Orchard (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lane, Diane', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN AIRLINES THEATER', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gevinson, Tavi', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karam, Stephen', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grey, Joel', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glover, John', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chekhov, Anton', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Godwin, Simon (Theater Director)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roundabout Theater Co', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-17T02:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/76836ced-f757-5b47-a07a-3732b3748cce', 'word_count': 980, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76836ced-f757-5b47-a07a-3732b3748cce'}, {'abstract': 'As some of the hosts go further astray, Dr. Ford makes a reminder to remember what — not who — they really are.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/arts/television/westworld-season-1-episode-3-recap.html', 'snippet': 'As some of the hosts go further astray, Dr. Ford makes a reminder to remember what — not who — they really are.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his Godlike capacity as the creative director and visionary behind Westworld, Dr. Ford has been tinkering with the mental capacities of the park’s android hosts, like adding “reveries” that draw on memories of previous constructs. The guests will appreciate these subtle touches more than the canned story lines that lead them on bounty-hunting adventures or to rendezvous with painted ladies. And yet this week’s episode suggests that Ford still wants the hosts to operate within certain cognitive limits, for their own good and for the good of the park. He wants to force them into the paradoxical position of remembering and forgetting at the same time: remembering enough to bring depth and nuance to their behavior, but forgetting the routine horrors of their existence at the end of the night.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/television/16westworld/16westworld-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/television/16westworld/16westworld-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/television/16westworld/16westworld-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/16/arts/television/16westworld/16westworld-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty