ere Selected for Death', 'kicker': 'Fiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Selected for Death', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Chosen Ones (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sem-Sandberg, Steve', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paterson, Anna (Translator)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-18T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Rubin Suleiman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': 'Rubin', 'lastname': 'Suleiman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdf27480-46fe-51c0-b9b0-d3a7a4e94848', 'word_count': 1246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdf27480-46fe-51c0-b9b0-d3a7a4e94848'}, {'abstract': 'One of this presidential election’s most profound disagreements is over what our unconscious judgments say, or don’t say, about who we are.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/23/magazine/how-bias-went-from-a-psychological-observation-to-a-political-accusation.html', 'snippet': 'One of this presidential election’s most profound disagreements is over what our unconscious judgments say, or don’t say, about who we are.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2004, 57 police officers of different races were divided into two groups for a simple experiment. Half of them were shown two photo lineups, one with an array of white faces and one with black faces. This group was more visually attuned to the white faces. A second group looked at the same lineups after words like “violent,” “crime” and “shoot” flashed on their screens, at the edge of their field of vision. This group of officers’ eyes were mostly drawn to the black faces. In a similar test, using pictures of guns and knives instead of words, a group of white college students exhibited a similar shift in attention.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23firstwords/23firstwords-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23firstwords/23firstwords-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23firstwords/23firstwords-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23firstwords/23firstwords-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23firstwords/23firstwords-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23firstwords/23firstwords-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23firstwords/23firstwords-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1772, 'width': 1772, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How ‘Bias’ Went From a Psychological Observation to a Political Accusation', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Basic Instinct', 'name': 