97d8a6', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e3bb3a2-fefb-5975-b7d7-b230d697d8a6'}, {'abstract': 'Chumley’s returns with a new menu, Flora Bar opens at the Met Breuer, and more restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/19/dining/tom-colicchio-fowler-and-wells-beekman-hotel-restaurant-news.html', 'snippet': 'Chumley’s returns with a new menu, Flora Bar opens at the Met Breuer, and more restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOWLER & WELLS The Beekman, a new hotel in a stunning landmark building steps from City Hall, has dining options that are bound to become downtown destinations. The atrium, a sprawling, comfortably upholstered bar and lounge, is flanked by this restaurant from Tom Colicchio, and another one, Augustine, from Keith McNally, that will open in a few weeks. Mr. Colicchio named his restaurant for two phrenologists who practiced their pseudoscience in the building. With his executive chef, Bryan Hunt, he has devised a seasonal menu with dishes like chicory salad with pears, candied walnuts and Roquefort cheese; sautéed foie gras with persimmons, pink peppercorns and bitter greens; and rabbit schnitzel with pistachios and lemon. Favorites from an earlier era, like lobster thermidor and beef Wellington, will be offered as specials. “I think of this as old New York modernized,” Mr. Colicchio said. The handsome brick-walled room with stained-glass windows combines polished vintage details with industrial elements. (Opens Thursday): 5 Beekman Street (Nassau Street), 212-658-1848, fowlerandwells.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/19/dining/19OFF/19OFF-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/19/dining/19OFF/19OFF-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}},