{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-19T09:01:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Max Fisher and Amanda Taub', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Max', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fisher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taub', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf9d6a72-940c-5691-9431-2c2eaace8cfe', 'word_count': 1407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf9d6a72-940c-5691-9431-2c2eaace8cfe'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on when to share information someone may not want to hear, whether you’re obliged to report a predatory professor and whether you can cast a vote for a relative with dementia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/23/magazine/should-you-tell-a-mans-fiancee-that-he-faked-his-degrees.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on when to share information someone may not want to hear, whether you’re obliged to report a predatory professor and whether you can cast a vote for a relative with dementia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago, I realized that a close friend was misrepresenting himself professionally as having multiple graduate degrees that he did not actually earn. When I confronted him in a compassionate way, he denied it, but could offer no evidence to the contrary. We have fallen out of touch, but he is now engaged to a woman I know. We have several mutual friends. I want nothing to do with this guy and have done my best to forget about his deception, but if I were his fiancée, I would certainly want to know. Thoughts? Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 398, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 398, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should You Tell a Man’s Fiancée That He Faked His Degrees?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Man I Know Faked His Academic Credentials. Should I Tell His Fiancée?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appiah, Kwame Anthony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lying', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-19T09:01:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab4590a3-3240-5dc1-bd98-e95525d577ca', 'word_count': 1363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab4590a3-3240-5dc1-bd98-e95525d577ca'}, {'abstract': 'The exposure of the Clinton campaign’s internal emails shows that a ubiquitous, and vulnerable, communication tool is ready for the scrap heap.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/20/technology/whoever-wins-the-white-house-this-years-big-loser-is-email.html', 'snippet': 'The exposure of the Clinton campaign’s internal emails shows that a ubiquitous, and vulnerable, communication tool is ready for the scrap heap.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every four years, pundits race to anoint this or that newfangled tech trend as the next disruptive force to forever alter the mechanics of American democracy. The 2016 campaign has already been called the Snapchat election, the Periscope election, the Meerkat election, the Twitter election, the Facebook election and the meme election. (If there were a vomit emoji, I’d insert one here. And then we’d have the emoji election.)', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/20/technology/20state-illo/20state-illo-thumbStanda