 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c872eff3-58f6-5730-93d0-634cbac5ef42', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c872eff3-58f6-5730-93d0-634cbac5ef42'}, {'abstract': 'For a show combining low-key naturalism and a steady helping of the surreal, you need actors who don’t seem as if they’re “working,” and this cast is one of the best on TV.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/23/arts/television/atlanta-has-brilliant-characters-and-some-of-the-best-acting-on-tv.html', 'snippet': 'For a show combining low-key naturalism and a steady helping of the surreal, you need actors who don’t seem as if they’re “working,” and this cast is one of the best on TV.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the presidential debates and at campaign rallies, Donald J. Trump has indulged a fondness for the equation of black life and hell: Happiness is scarce, and misery, poverty and violence afflict all. That’s a certain white man’s view of black life, as seen on his TV set — in 1989, when the Huxtables were the only prominent African-Americans visible amid proliferating news images of “dangerous” black people. Television in 2016, with its bounty of black shows, both rebukes and complicates that dehumanizing assessment. And the show doing that with the most farcical tang, at the moment, is “Atlanta,” now in its first season on FX.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/arts/23ATLANTACOVER/23ATLANTACOVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/23/arts/23ATLANTACOVER/23ATLANTACOVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/arts/23ATLANTAC