one}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e4b63f7-654d-562a-a532-62982cd11d73', 'word_count': 1017, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e4b63f7-654d-562a-a532-62982cd11d73'}, {'abstract': 'Accusations against Saudi Arabia and Salafi theology distract us from the real causes of violent extremism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/20/opinion/dont-blame-wahhabism-for-terrorism.html', 'snippet': 'Accusations against Saudi Arabia and Salafi theology distract us from the real causes of violent extremism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The word “Wahhabism” has become a boogeyman in the West, deemed responsible for the radicalization of Muslims around the world. And since Wahhabism is a strain of Islam that has its origins in the Arabian Peninsula and is the dominant religious doctrine of Saudi Arabia, that country is often viewed as the prime culprit in the propagation of violent extremism.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Blame ‘Wahhabism’ for Terrorism', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t link ‘Wahhabism’ to terrorism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abdul-Wahhab, Muhammad ibn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-19T12:50:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohammed Alyahya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohammed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alyahya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5198e45-5ae6-5c1b-94f2-9c31556a5295', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5198e45-5ae6-5c1b-94f2-9c31556a5295'}, {'abstract': 'Water conservation has declined since the state lifted a mandatory 25 percent cut after a relatively wet winter. And the drought has not gone away.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/20/us/as-drought-california-water-use-rises-some-ask-were-limits-eased-too-soon.html', 'snippet': 'Water conservation has declined since the state lifted a mandatory 25 percent cut after a relatively wet winter. And the drought has not gone away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST PORTERVILLE, Calif. — This state slashed urban water use over 25 percent in the face of a punishing drought last year, exceeding a mandatory order issued by Gov. Jerry Brown and turning California into a model of water conservation. Californians tore out lawns, cut back landscape watering and took shorter showers as they embraced Mr. Brown’s call to accommodate what he warned were permanently drier times.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/20/us/20drought-1/00drought1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/20/us/20drought-1/00drought1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/20/us/20drought-1/00drought1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/20/us/20drought-1/00drought1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_nam