 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22765a63-a297-5a95-96e9-2660895d3cbc'}, {'abstract': 'The Israeli minister of culture and sports wants nothing less than an overthrow of the nation’s elite.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/23/magazine/miri-regevs-culture-war.html', 'snippet': 'The Israeli minister of culture and sports wants nothing less than an overthrow of the nation’s elite.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Tel Aviv Museum of art is a somber slab of concrete built in the no-fuss brutalist style of the late ’60s. The architect who planned the museum also designed Israel’s nuclear reactor; such is the overlap between culture and political exigency in the country. One Sunday in March, a throng of artists, actors and writers clustered in the museum’s main auditorium. They were awaiting the arrival of Miri Regev, Israel’s culture and sports minister, of the ruling Likud Party. A year earlier, Benjamin Netanyahu, Israel’s prime minister and the chairman of Likud, secured a fourth term in office and swore in his ministers, a coalition widely regarded as the most right-wing in Israeli history.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23regev1/23regev1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23regev1/23regev1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23regev1/23regev1-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/magazine/23regev1/23regev1-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width