N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Falls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-20T11:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Live', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sylvia Rupani-Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sylvia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rupani-Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cfcb99b-f906-566e-a561-bd60e009f666', 'word_count': 948, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cfcb99b-f906-566e-a561-bd60e009f666'}, {'abstract': 'There’s not a single dud among the 39 works in a show at the Metropolitan Museum of Art about this Expressionist painter’s special connection to the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/21/arts/design/review-max-beckmann-in-new-york-a-belated-but-full-blown-homage-to-a-german-modernist.html', 'snippet': 'There’s not a single dud among the 39 works in a show at the Metropolitan Museum of Art about this Expressionist painter’s special connection to the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the years of the Weimar Republic (1919-33), the Expressionist painter Max Beckmann was one of Germany’s most celebrated living artists. Then the Nazis designated him a degenerate artist and began confiscating his works. He and his wife, Mathilde, known as Quappi, left Germany in 1937 and moved to Amsterdam, where they stayed until 1947, when they were finally granted a visa to come to the United States. Here he remained, for two years teaching at Washington University in St. Louis, and for his last years, until his death in 1950, residing in New York City.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/21/arts/21BECKMANN1/21BECKMANN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 920, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge',