lifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f80a5623-0415-5bb6-8e3b-32ad62eec57a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f80a5623-0415-5bb6-8e3b-32ad62eec57a'}, {'abstract': 'This film about a spy couple has star power in Jon Hamm, but utilitarian construction and a lack of chemistry are downfalls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/21/movies/keeping-up-with-the-joneses-review.html', 'snippet': 'This film about a spy couple has star power in Jon Hamm, but utilitarian construction and a lack of chemistry are downfalls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One way to end the scourge of “TV is better than film” articles is to stop making movies like “Keeping Up With the Joneses,” a pedestrian comedy that almost seems intended as evidence for the cause. The plot invites comparisons to “The Americans”; the presence of Jon Hamm as a man of mystery prompts wistful memories of “Mad Men”; and the visual vocabulary — no one has bothered to address the abundance of overlit and sun-bleached shots — shows the indifference of a hasty live broadcast.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/21/arts/21KEEPING/21KEEPING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 459, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/21/arts/21KEEPING/21KEEPING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 459}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/21/arts/21KEEPING/21KEEPING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/21/arts/21KEEPING/21KEEPING-superJumbo.jpg', 'height