erson': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7aef5961-0666-5369-a84d-ec13aa4b1bf5', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7aef5961-0666-5369-a84d-ec13aa4b1bf5'}, {'abstract': 'Donald Trump’s epithet, as usual, sticks — but not the way he intended it to.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/20/magazine/the-nickname-hillary-clinton-has-been-waiting-for.html', 'snippet': 'Donald Trump’s epithet, as usual, sticks — but not the way he intended it to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You could practically see the T-shirts emblazoned with the phrase before it left Donald Trump’s mouth on Wednesday night: “Such a nasty woman.” The hashtag #nastywoman started trending almost immediately on Twitter. “From one nasty woman to another, you were an inspiration last night,” Nancy Pelosi tweeted at Clinton. “RT if you’re a nasty woman and it’s made your life a freakin’ pleasure,” wrote Lena Dunham; by midday Thursday, 3,000 or so women had done just that.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/20/magazine/20mag-clinton-1/20mag-hillary-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/20/magazine/20mag-clinton-1/20mag-hillary-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/20/magazine/20mag-clinton-1/20mag-hillary-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/20/magazine/20mag-clinton-1/20mag-hillary-superJumbo.jpg', '