act': 'Bryan Cranston, whose memoir is No. 9 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has a sense of humor about his fame: “It’s a little .\u2008.\u2008. odd to have your face tattooed on someone’s backside.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/books/review/the-story-behind-this-weeks-best-sellers.html', 'snippet': 'Bryan Cranston, whose memoir is No. 9 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has a sense of humor about his fame: “It’s a little .\u2008.\u2008. odd to have your face tattooed on someone’s backside.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ink: Before Bryan Cranston became a TV star, as the father on “Malcolm in the Middle” and especially as Walter White in “Breaking Bad,” he spent decades just trying to make a living as an actor, hustling for bit parts and homely commercial work. To judge from the genial, disarming tone of his memoir, “A Life in Parts” (new at No.\u202f9 on the hardcover nonfiction list), that journeyman experience has given him the perspective to stay humble in the face of fame — or at least to maintain a sense of humor about it. “I’ve seen ‘Breaking Bad’ T-shirts in cities and little outposts all over the world,” he writes toward the end of the book. “People dress as Walter White for Halloween. They dress their children and dogs in hazmat suits and Heisenberg hats. People have shaved my face into the back of their heads, they’ve inked it on their backs, their forearms, their legs, their asses. I’m the permanent resident of some guy’s left butt cheek. Next to Larry and Moe. I suppose I’ve taken the place of Curly, the third Stooge. It’s a little .\u2008.\u2008. odd to have your face tattooed on someone’s backside. I did not see that coming when I lied about my ability to mountaineer in order to get a Mars bar commercial.” Fame has other pitfalls, too, Cranston adds. “As an actor you need to be sensitive, to be open, to be able to observe people and study human behavior,” he writes. “But as a celebrity I am no longer the observer, I am the observed. I am not the one who knocks. I am the one who ducks and covers.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23list-cranston/23list-cranston-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23list-cranston/23list-cranston-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23list-cranston/23list-cranston-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23list-cranston/23list-cranston-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23list-cranston/23list-cranston-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23list-cranston/23list-cranston-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23list-cranston/23list-cranston-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Story Behind This Week’s Best Sellers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cranston, Bryan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-21T15:18:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/5165875b-e1a6-57e5-9067-2c01d1eddcba', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5165875b-e1a6-57e5-9067-2c01d1eddcba'}, {'abstract': 'Seven new paperbacks to check out this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/23/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Seven new paperbacks to check out this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seven new paperbacks to check out this week.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23paperrow/23paperrow-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 296, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23paperrow/23paperrow-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 296}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23paperrow/23paperrow-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 784, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/23/books/review/23paperrow/23paperrow-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 784, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im