, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-10-24T07:30:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a8adec3-e598-51a3-a486-878b8ef13ced', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a8adec3-e598-51a3-a486-878b8ef13ced'}, {'abstract': 'In “Stolen Land, Stolen Futures,” Michael Santiago looks at the challenges facing a third-generation black farmer as he struggles to stave off foreclosure and hold on to his land.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/10/24/a-difficult-harvest-for-americas-black-farmers-michael-santiago/', 'snippet': 'In “Stolen Land, Stolen Futures,” Michael Santiago looks at the challenges facing a third-generation black farmer as he struggles to stave off foreclosure and hold on to his land.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Santiago spent years working at dead-end retail and warehouse jobs before he decided to take a chance and pursue photography. Mr. Santiago, a New York native, went to study in California, where his biggest lesson came not in a classroom or studio, but on a pig farm run by James McGill, a third-generation African-American farmer who has struggled to keep his land amid looming threats of foreclosure.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Difficult Harvest for America’s Black Farmers', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value':