[{'firstname': 'Anemona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HARTOCOLLIS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Madison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MILLS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ORME', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/736d8576-a112-531a-a301-f660da51a3a8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/736d8576-a112-531a-a301-f660da51a3a8'}, {'abstract': 'Franny Moyle’s ambitious biography depicts J.M.W. Turner’s talent, work ethic and self-involvement in detail but with little style or warmth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/26/books/review-turner-a-portrait-of-a-most-dazzling-and-disagreeable-artist.html', 'snippet': 'Franny Moyle’s ambitious biography depicts J.M.W. Turner’s talent, work ethic and self-involvement in detail but with little style or warmth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most cloying American artist of the 20th century was almost certainly Thomas Kinkade (1958-2012), whose popular paintings depicted what seemed to be well-lighted houses for elves. His work, as Joan Didion wrote, “typically featured a cottage or a house of such insistent coziness as to seem actually sinister, suggestive of a trap designed to attract Hansel and Gretel.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/26/arts/26BOOKMOYLE1/26BOOKTURNER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/26/arts/26BOOKMOYLE1/26BOOKTURNER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 733}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cre