riginal': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92ab0cd0-ba98-5ab8-a8cb-d3d7a37bbc55', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92ab0cd0-ba98-5ab8-a8cb-d3d7a37bbc55'}, {'abstract': 'Leo Braudy’s “Haunted” probes the cultural and historical origins of ghosts, witches, vampires and zombies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/books/review/haunted-leo-braudy.html', 'snippet': 'Leo Braudy’s “Haunted” probes the cultural and historical origins of ghosts, witches, vampires and zombies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HAUNTEDOn Ghosts, Witches, Vampires, Zombies, and Other Monsters of the Natural and Supernatural WorldsBy Leo BraudyIllustrated. 306 pp. Yale University Press. $30.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/books/review/30maguire-sub-1/30maguire-sub-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/30/books/review/30maguire-sub-1/30maguire-sub-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/books/review/30maguire-sub-1/30maguire-sub-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 584, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/books/review/30maguire-sub-1/30maguire-sub-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 584, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Time for Halloween, a Taxonomy of Monsters', 'kicker': 'Nonfiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where the Wild Things Come From', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Haunted: On Ghosts, Witches, Vampires, Zombies, and Other Monsters of the Natural and Supernatural Worlds (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Braudy, Leo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-26T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Maguire', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maguire', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/06200c18-da8c-5e1f-a35f-7d8fe6c765fd', 'word_count': 1299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06200c18-da8c-5e1f-a35f-7d8fe6c765fd'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on what to do when your ex decides to treat her body as a commodity, whether to employ a parolee and if you should tell a potential employer you’ve been fired.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/magazine/my-ex-is-advertising-for-sugar-daddies-can-i-tell-her-mother.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on what to do when your ex decides to treat her body as a commodity, whether to employ a parolee and if you should tell a potential employer you’ve been fired.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I recently broke up with a longtime girlfriend. We are both in our mid-20s. She was temporarily without work for the summer and low on cash. After our breakup, it came to my attention that she had made an account on a “dating” website and had been messaging much older men about, basically, exchanging herself for money. Establishing “relationships” like these is in vogue among girls in our age bracket who seek to pay off college loans or take exotic Instagrammable vacations on someone else’s dime. Yes, I understand it’s 2016, and women are empowered to do what they wish with their bodies, but I fear that these men are probably unstable (most likely married, let’s be honest) and definitely do not have her best interests at heart. Maybe it’s watching too much cable news, but I fear for her safety. I considered bringing it to the attention of her mother, who I know will not be supportive of this line of employment, but I’m conflicted. I know it’s not my place, but if something were to happen to her and I had this information at my disposal beforehand, wouldn’t I be in the wrong? Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30ethicist/30ethicist-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 839, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30ethicist/30ethicist-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 839}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30ethicist/30ethicist-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 733, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30ethicist/30ethicist-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1403, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30ethicist/30ethicist-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30ethicist/30ethicist-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30ethicist/30ethicist-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Ex Is Advertising for Sugar Daddies. Can I Tell Her Mother?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Ex Is Advertising for Sugar Daddies. Can I tell Her Mother?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appiah, Kwame Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Relationships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Probation and Parole', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-26T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b72cdc4-a6bd-5b20-b976-996bfed42cf7', 'word_count': 1826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b72cdc4-a6bd-5b20-b976-996bfed42cf7'}, {'abstract': 'People call them old-man cars — but what’s so bad about growing up?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-buicks.html', 'snippet': 'People call them old-man cars — but what’s so bad about growing up?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The moment my father pulled into the driveway with his new Buick LeSabre Limited was one of his proudest. He parked the car in the center of our circular driveway, right by the front door, and beckoned my mother and me to come have a look. The LeSabre was a beautiful car, the color of Champagne. It had a tufted velour interior, soft vinyl doors and a faux-walnut dashboard that lit up warm as a hearth.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30lor/30lor-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30lor/30lor-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30lor/30lor-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30lor/30lor-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30lor/30lor-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30lor/30lor-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/magazine/30lor/30lor-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1449, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter of Recommendation: Buicks', 'kicker': 'Let