None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fddbe8a7-7d5d-566c-936b-02847ae9e84c', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fddbe8a7-7d5d-566c-936b-02847ae9e84c'}, {'abstract': 'Danspace’s “Platform 2016: Lost and Found” highlights a program dedicated to the work of John Bernd, who died in 1988.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/arts/dance/danspace-project-remembers-the-lost-unknown-ghosts-of-aids.html', 'snippet': 'Danspace’s “Platform 2016: Lost and Found” highlights a program dedicated to the work of John Bernd, who died in 1988.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Danspace Project delves deeper into its “Platform 2016: Lost and Found,” which looks at the effect that AIDS has had on generations of dance artists, it features a program dedicated to the work of John Bernd, who died in 1988. One of the first choreographers to incorporate gay themes and AIDS into his work, Mr. Bernd will be honored in “Variations on Themes from ‘Lost and Found’: Scenes from a Life and Other Works by John Bernd” beginning Thursday, Nov. 3. Ishmael Houston-Jones, who along with Will Rawls organized “Lost and Found,” directs the work, which features a cast of seven young dancers as well as the collaboration of Jennifer Monson (who danced with Mr. Bernd in “Two on the Loose,” his last performance before he died), Nick Hallett and Miguel Gutierrez.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEADSUB8/30WEEKAHEADSUB8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEADSUB8/30WEEKAHEADSUB8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEADSUB8/30WEEKAHEADSUB8-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEADSUB8/30WEEKAHEADSUB8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEADSUB8/30WEEKAHEADSUB8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEADSUB8/30WEEKAHEADSUB8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEADSUB8/30WEEKAHEADSUB8-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Danspace Project Remembers the Lost Unknown Ghosts of AIDS', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance; Remembering Ghosts of AIDS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Danspace Project', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernd, John (1953-88)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Houston-Jones, Ishmael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rawls, Will J (1978- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-26T16:43:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f5bd82a-48ee-527f-b8c9-dbec5bc95c0c', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f5bd82a-48ee-527f-b8c9-dbec5bc95c0c'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Beatty’s “Rules Don’t Apply” is the first film he has written, directed and starred in since “Bulworth,” in 1998. And he’ll talk your ear off about it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/movies/warren-beatty-rules-dont-apply.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Beatty’s “Rules Don’t Apply” is the first film he has written, directed and starred in since “Bulworth,” in 1998. And he’ll talk your ear off about it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Lest there be any question about whether, at 79, Warren Beatty is still a force of nature, the interview for this article lasted more than six hours. Mr. Beatty could have kept going, but I, in exhaustion, begged off. “As you have learned,” Mr. Beatty said, as we emerged on a golden midsummer evening from his Beverly Hills mansion, where we had spent most of the day, “I bleed people dry.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30BEATTYJP1/30BEATTYJP1-thumbStandard-v7.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30BEATTYJP1/30BEATTYJP1-thumbStandard-v7.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30BEATTYJP1/30BEATTYJP1-thumbLarge-v7.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30BEATTYJP1/30BEATTYJP1-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30BEATTYJP1/30BEATTYJP1-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30BEATTYJP1/30BEATTYJP1-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30BEATTYJP1/30BEATTYJP1-superJumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1638, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Warren Beatty Wants Six Hours of Your Time. Or More.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking His Own Sweet Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Rules Don't Apply (Movie)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beatty, Warren', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bening, Annette', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ehrenreich, Alden', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Lily', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-26T16:47:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81797719-fbc8-54d9-93b9-892efb5ee0bd', 'word_count': 1747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81797719-fbc8-54d9-93b9-892efb5ee0bd'}, {'abstract': 'In the British series “Brief Encounters,” it’s the 1980s, and would-be saleswomen take on sexual repression — or something like that.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/arts/television/picture-a-tupperware-party-but-with-racier-products.html', 'snippet': 'In the British series “Brief Encounters,” it’s the 1980s, and would-be saleswomen take on sexual repression — or something like that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s 1982, and times are hard in England. How does a woman help shore up the family finances? In the delicious British dramedy “Brief Encounters,” which begins streaming Monday, Oct. 31, on Acorn, the answer for several entrepreneurs is selling lingerie and sex toys to repressed women at Tupperware-like parties. The six-episode series may feel more 1950s than 1980s, but the results are often hilarious. “Allow me to introduce the Stallion,” one budding saleswomen says at her first party as she pulls a formidable marital aid from her bag. The prim-and-proper guests look on, stunned, until one asks, “Is that a food blender?” It isn’t. Yet there are plenty of serious moments, too: When they’re not peddling their products, these women face marital strife, social ostracism and more.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEAD2/30WEEKAHEAD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEAD2/30WEEKAHEAD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEAD2/30WEEKAHEAD2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, '