': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9ad8f0d-3370-593c-ab9c-f0323c1e58da', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9ad8f0d-3370-593c-ab9c-f0323c1e58da'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Chenoweth starts a series of concert performances at the Lunt-Fontanne Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/theater/kristin-chenoweths-many-lullabies-for-broadway.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Chenoweth starts a series of concert performances at the Lunt-Fontanne Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kristin Chenoweth, a Broadway baby if ever there was one, returns to the boards she’s trod off and on for almost 20 years in a series of concert performances at the Lunt-Fontanne Theater. But she hasn’t been away for long. Ever since she blazed onto Broadway with a Tony-winning, show-stealing performance in a revival of “You’re a Good Man, Charlie Brown” in 1999 (she officially made her Broadway debut two years earlier, in “Steel Pier”), Ms. Chenoweth has returned regularly, in revivals of “Promises, Promises” and “On the Twentieth Century,” but perhaps most memorably as the perkier witch in the long-running “Wicked,” chirping merrily about her popularity.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEAD6/30WEEKAHEAD6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEAD6/30WEEKAHEAD6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEAD6/30WEEKAHEAD6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'heigh