he Crowd,” “All the King’s Men,” “Citizen Kane” and more, pegged to the presidential election.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/movies/demagogues-presents-political-kings-and-kingmakers.html', 'snippet': 'A Film Forum series includes “A Face in the Crowd,” “All the King’s Men,” “Citizen Kane” and more, pegged to the presidential election.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cast a vote for insight at Film Forum, where the programmer Bruce Goldstein has lined up masterworks timed to the election. “Demagogues” (Nov. 4 to Nov. 11) offers the obvious like “Citizen Kane” and “All the King’s Men” and curiosities like Gregory La Cava’s “Gabriel Over the White House,” from 1933, with Walter Huston as a president who becomes a benevolent dictator after a car crash. Most devastating is Elia Kazan’s “A Face in the Crowd” (1957), which reunited Kazan and his “On the Waterfront” screenwriter, Budd Schulberg. Lonesome Rhodes, a charismatic Southern singing jailbird (Andy Griffith, surpassing his television roles), is groomed into a TV star by a reporter (Patricia Neal), eventually becoming a political kingmaker felled by his libido, raging hypocrisy and hollow populism. A mentor warns him about “the way you begin to shoot suddenly out of control,” but the seeds of Rhodes’s undoing appear the moment the reporter first asks him to sing. “Wait a minute,” he says. “What do I get out of this?” (filmforum.org)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEAD7/30WEEKAHEAD7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 513, 'width': 338, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/arts/30WEEKAHEAD7/30WEEKAHEAD7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 513, 'width': 338, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, 