: 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Jean-pierre', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landau', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cda571f-3cdd-5ffe-a5e0-87512b75516c', 'word_count': 1022, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cda571f-3cdd-5ffe-a5e0-87512b75516c'}, {'abstract': 'We discuss our recent magazine pieces: Wesley’s about penises in popular culture and Jenna’s about Barack Obama as the first digital-age president.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/27/podcasts/black-sexuality.html', 'snippet': 'We discuss our recent magazine pieces: Wesley’s about penises in popular culture and Jenna’s about Barack Obama as the first digital-age president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Popular culture is having something of a male nudity breakthrough. In the last couple of years, there has been a surprising boomlet of penises on television and at the movies. Although it begins (barely) to catch men up to decades of topless (and bottomless) women, there’s still inequality at play: The penises deemed safe enough to see tend to be white ones. Seeing a black penis is rare. So is experiencing healthy black sexuality in popular culture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/27/arts/27processing/27processing-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/27/arts/27processing/27processing-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/27/arts/27processing/27processing-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge',