le': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b93e2c1-2c31-519a-b2c0-d90cef58b744', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b93e2c1-2c31-519a-b2c0-d90cef58b744'}, {'abstract': 'Upending misogynistic tribal codes is the real key to finally ending the most egregious gender crime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/28/opinion/pakistans-onor-killing-law-isnt-enough.html', 'snippet': 'Upending misogynistic tribal codes is the real key to finally ending the most egregious gender crime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KARACHI, Pakistan — In Pakistan today, it’s a sad reality that regressive societal attitudes toward women label us as commodities, second-class citizens and financial liabilities to our families. This leaves us open to abusive and violent traditions, dictated by tribal codes and enforced by social and religious conservatism: child marriages, forced marriages, bartering of women to appease feuds and the most egregious gender crime, honor killings.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/shah-contributor/shah-contributor-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 940, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/shah-contributor/shah-contributor-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 940}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/shah-contributor/shah-contributor-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 654, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/shah-contributor/shah-contributor-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'wid