 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0017883c-eaba-508f-a632-3187917643ce', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0017883c-eaba-508f-a632-3187917643ce'}, {'abstract': 'He was a bit too brief in his OkCupid overture, but love wins with song.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/fashion/weddings/genevieve-debose-and-tosin-akinnagbe-hes-no-shakespeare-romeo-maybe.html', 'snippet': 'He was a bit too brief in his OkCupid overture, but love wins with song.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Geneviève DeBose were something other than a seventh- and eighth-grade English teacher, she might have been less dismissive of the all-too-brief message she received from Tosin Akinnagbe via OkCupid in September 2015.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Geneviève DeBose and Tosin Akinnagbe: He’s No Shakespeare. Romeo? Maybe.', 'kicker': 'Vows', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'He’s No Shakespeare. But Romeo? Maybe.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Dating', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bloomberg LP', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'OkCupid', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vows (Times Column)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-28T10:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tammy La Gorce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tammy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Gorce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d20eb6d-06ee-52f2-a6c1-f3e2d897507c', 'word_count': 1560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d20eb6d-06ee-52f2-a6c1-f3e2d897507c'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Ackroyd’s life of Alfred Hitchcock reveals the vulnerabilities beneath the calculating exterior.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/books/review/alfred-hitchcock-biography-peter-ackroyd.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Ackroyd’s life of Alfred Hitchcock reveals the vulnerabilities beneath the calculating exterior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These are good times for Alfred Hitchcock. The refurbishment of the director’s reputation, which began in 1966 when François Truffaut published his landmark book of interviews, “Hitchcock/Truffaut,” reached its conclusion in 2012 when the film critics polled by Sight and Sound voted “Vertigo” the greatest film of all time, kicking Orson Welles’s “Citizen Kane” from a top spot it had enjoyed for decades. Wellesians bit their knuckles, and the rest of us scratched our heads. “Vertigo” is not Hitchcock’s best, but rather, with its lush morbidity, somnolent pace, poor box office and relative scarcity of jokes, the Hitchcock film for those who most wish he were French. Flops make film critics feel useful — they are the film-crit equivalent of the deserving poor. What else can you do with a gleaming hit maker except overpraise his misses?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/24/books/review/30SHONE24-INYT/30SHONE-Hitchcock-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/24/books/review/30SHONE24-INYT/30SHONE-Hitchcock-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/24/books/review/30SHONE24-INYT/30SHONE-Hitchcock-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'ju