{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-28T22:01:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hawes Spencer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hawes', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spencer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f83dce9b-9486-54d4-bbf1-e3424ea81e47', 'word_count': 552, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f83dce9b-9486-54d4-bbf1-e3424ea81e47'}, {'abstract': 'In an era of climate change, cities no longer look like the problem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/the-kind-of-thinking-cities-need.html', 'snippet': 'In an era of climate change, cities no longer look like the problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Quito, Ecuador — Day after day, under a bright sun, hourslong security lines snaked out of El Ejido Park into the surrounding streets. Enterprising women in multicolored skirts and brown hats sold water and folding umbrellas for shade. Inside, representatives at the German pavilion seduced passers-by with beer and apples to spread propaganda on the “circular economy,’’ while the incongruous sound of a mariachi band playing Frank Sinatra music wafted from Ecuador’s booth, nearly drowning out a speech by an architect from California describing her company’s projects to improve a slum in Kenya.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30kimmelman/30kimmelman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30kimmelman/30kimmelman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0