, 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1a9ceb7-c33a-5ecc-ae48-f808a50be4a2', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1a9ceb7-c33a-5ecc-ae48-f808a50be4a2'}, {'abstract': 'At home and abroad, some risks are fairly easy to predict, but they’re not the worst-case scenarios.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/the-dangers-of-donald-trump.html', 'snippet': 'At home and abroad, some risks are fairly easy to predict, but they’re not the worst-case scenarios.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN last Sunday’s column, I wrote about the dangers that a Hillary Clinton presidency poses for the country. Since this is the final week for Hillary skeptics to agonize over whether to cast a vote for Donald Trump, it seems appropriate to outline why the risks of Trump are so distinctive as to throw the perils of a Clinton presidency into relative eclipse.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30DOUTHAT-web/30DOUTHAT-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30DOUTHAT-web/30DOUTHAT-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30DOUTHAT-web/30DOUTHAT-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30DOUTHAT-web/30DOUTHAT-web-