, Bill', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-29T19:10:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Faludi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Faludi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d78a8d2-cbd7-507f-9fb3-8c4a5af0a0bd', 'word_count': 1093, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d78a8d2-cbd7-507f-9fb3-8c4a5af0a0bd'}, {'abstract': 'The poverty rate has improved, but inequality is still dragging our economy down.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/richer-but-not-better-off.html', 'snippet': 'The poverty rate has improved, but inequality is still dragging our economy down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been a long, painful slog out of the Great Recession. More than seven years into a halting recovery, fewer than 60 percent of working-age Americans hold a job — not much more than at the trough of the recession in the spring of 2009.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Richer but Not Better Off', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Richer But Not Better Off', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Porter, Eduardo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Census Bureau', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infant Mortality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Longevity', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-29T19:17:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eduardo Porter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eduardo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Porter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/4221cd5e-9a87-549b-97e3-e2fa805935bd', 'word_count': 1147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4221cd5e-9a87-549b-97e3-e2fa805935bd'}, {'abstract': 'What Clinton vs. Trump feels like for first-time voters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/opinion/campaign-stops/i-registered-to-votefor-this.html', 'snippet': 'What Clinton vs. Trump feels like for first-time voters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this month, the United States hit a new benchmark in an already historic presidential campaign: For the first time in the country’s history, 200 million Americans are registered to vote. Over the past eight years, more than 50 million people have registered. That could mean that more Americans are voting for the first time this year than ever before.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/30roller-together/30roller-together-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/30roller-together/30roller-together-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/30roller-together/30roller-together-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/30roller-together/30roller-together-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1225, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/30roller-together/30roller-together-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/30roller-together/30roller-together-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/30roller-together/30roller-together-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Registered to Vote... For This?', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Registered to Vote…for This?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roller, Emma', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-29T19:18:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Campaign Stops', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma Roller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5001ee3f-59b4-5669-b1c2-c2cccbbf27bd', 'word_count': 1680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5001ee3f-59b4-5669-b1c2-c2cccbbf27bd'}, {'abstract': 'The same forces that propel a radical candidate to a party’s nomination also provide a floor through which he is unlikely to fall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/opinion/campaign-stops/why-trump-stays-afloat.html', 'snippet': 'The same forces that propel a radical candidate to a party’s nomination also provide a floor through which he is unlikely to fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one of the most emotionally wrenching presidential races in living memory, Donald J. Trump’s support is as level as a pond. In polls, it is holding steady around 41 percent, right where it was after the first debate in late September. This plateau has persisted through the second and third debates with his opponent, Hillary Clinton, and the revelation of his recorded boasts of sexual assault. Even the stunning public rebuke of Mr. Trump by the parents of the Iraq war hero Capt. Humayun Khan in late July was followed by a swing of just a few percentage points, quite small by historical standards. Why has Mr. Trump’s support not collapsed further in the face of some fairly damning revelations?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Trump Stays Afloat', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Trump Stays Afloat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wang, Sam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-29T19:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Campaign Stops', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Wang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/204ed758-c4de-5271-97c2-7f4fcfc5b6ca', 'word_count': 1283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/204ed758-c4de-5271-97c2-7f4fcfc5b6ca'}, {'abstract': 'First, Barcelona banned it. Now, Madrid says it must go on.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/spains-bullfighting-fight.html', 'snippet': 'First, Barcelona banned it. Now, Madrid says it must go on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADRID — When the Parliament of Catalonia, an autonomous region in northeastern Spain, solemnly banned bullfighting in 2010, it was not simply a victory for animal rights. There was a political angle, as well, involving a battle over regional and national identity.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30murado/30murado-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30murado/30murado-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30murado/30murado-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 632, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/30/opinion/sunday/30murado/30murado-superJumbo.