', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-10-31T16:44:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/798e2168-7b30-528d-8450-654a39863d25', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/798e2168-7b30-528d-8450-654a39863d25'}, {'abstract': 'Il Buco Alimentari e Vineria is selling the heirloom grains in NoLIta.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/02/dining/anson-mills-il-buco-alimentari-e-vineria-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'Il Buco Alimentari e Vineria is selling the heirloom grains in NoLIta.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For cornmeal, farro, oats or other grains from Anson Mills, the Columbia, S.C., company renowned for its high-quality heirloom products, you used to place your order on the website. But now, a few of the items, including popping corn and cornmeal for polenta, are being sold at the retail counter at Il Buco Alimentari e Vineria, in the East Village. The new baker on the premises, Sheena Otto (below), who was at Bien Cuit, is introducing some breads using Anson Mills grains, including a crusty dark rye and a buckwheat rye. Ms. Otto, who believes in a slow fermentation with no added yeast, is also baking a complex seed-studded multigrain in two sizes, and fragrant bourbon and raisin bread: Anson Mills products, $5.50 to $8; new breads, $6 to $12. Il Buco Alimentari e Vineria, 53 Great Jones Street (Bowery), 212-837-2622, ilbucovineria.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/11/02/dining/02burnergrains/02burnergrains-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType